Prevents a user-visible warning on every compilation:

  ld: warning: directory not found for option '-L.../MacOSX11.0.sdk/usr/lib/swift'

Only the *linker search path* is suppressed. This patch used to drop
`<sdk>/usr/lib/swift` from the module import paths as well, which is a different
list and unrelated to the warning above. That was harmless up to Swift 5.10,
because the toolchain built and shipped its own `Darwin` overlay, but Swift 6
removed the Darwin overlay from the swift repo entirely (there is no `swiftDarwin`
target in `stdlib/public/Platform/CMakeLists.txt` any more). The tgmath overloads
for `Float` now live only in the SDK — in `Darwin.swiftmodule` on the macOS 14
SDK, and in `_math.swiftmodule` from the macOS 15 SDK onwards — so hiding that
directory made `import Darwin.C.tgmath` resolve to the C declarations alone.
Every `Float` overload then fell back to its `Double` counterpart and
`stdlib/public/Differentiation/TgmathDerivatives.swift.gyb` failed to compile
with several hundred type errors.

Note the ordering: the SDK path is appended *after* the toolchain's own runtime
library import paths, so the freshly built stdlib still wins and only modules the
toolchain does not provide are picked up from the SDK.

--- a/lib/Driver/ToolChains.cpp
+++ b/lib/Driver/ToolChains.cpp
@@ -1685,9 +1685,11 @@ void ToolChain::getRuntimeLibraryPaths(SmallVectorImpl<std::string> &runtimeLibP
       runtimeLibPaths.push_back(std::string(scratchPath.str()));
     }

+    if (!SDKPath.starts_with("@storeDir@")) {
     scratchPath = SDKPath;
     llvm::sys::path::append(scratchPath, "usr", "lib", "swift");
     runtimeLibPaths.push_back(std::string(scratchPath.str()));
+    }
   }
 }

